# Copy to .env for local work. Every variable here is read at BUILD time and baked
# into the output, so changing one means rebuilding, and none of them is a secret:
# whatever is set is readable in the shipped JavaScript. Nothing else in the app
# reads the environment.
#
# All four are unset in this repo, and every page is built to degrade honestly
# without them rather than to fail: the waitlist reports that it could not send,
# and the credits page says it cannot take payment.

# Where the waitlist form POSTs {email, audience}. A third-party form endpoint
# (Formspree-style); no server of ours is involved. Unset, `submitWaitlist` returns
# "failed" rather than pretending to have sent — so an unconfigured deploy of the
# spec §7 Phase 0 pages collects nothing while looking fine. Set this before posting
# the launch links.
#
# Verify it once after setting it. `submitWaitlist` now reads the response body and
# not just the status, so a wrong URL that resolves to an SPA shell, or a form
# provider answering 200 with {"errors":[...]}, both show the developer "Couldn't
# send" instead of "You're on the list" — but neither tells *you* anything. The
# check is: submit a real address on the built page, then look for it in the form
# provider's own inbox. A green form is not evidence.
VITE_WAITLIST_ENDPOINT=https://formspree.io/f/replace-me

# Paddle's client-side token and environment, for /app/credits. Anything that is
# not exactly `production` is treated as the sandbox: an environment the app cannot
# read is one where real cards must not be charged. An empty token disables the buy
# button with a message rather than opening a checkout that cannot complete.
VITE_PADDLE_TOKEN=test_replace-me
VITE_PADDLE_ENV=sandbox

# The origin the prerendered pages name in og:url. Defaults to https://tickover.dev.
#
# THIS ONE IS NOT READ FROM THIS FILE. Measured: with VITE_SITE_URL in .env, a build
# still emits og:url="https://tickover.dev/developers". Vite loads .env into
# `import.meta.env` for the browser bundle, and this variable is not read there —
# `scripts/postbuild.ts` runs as a separate `tsx` process and reads `process.env`,
# which .env never reaches. Pass it in the environment instead:
#
#     VITE_SITE_URL=https://staging.example pnpm --filter @tickover/web build   # bash
#     $env:VITE_SITE_URL='https://staging.example'; pnpm --filter @tickover/web build   # PowerShell
#
# Left in this file as a comment on purpose: an uncommented line here would look
# like it worked, and the only symptom is every link preview naming the wrong site.
#VITE_SITE_URL=https://tickover.dev
